GUIDES = [
dict(slug="cost-of-24-hour-care", cat="Costs & Paying", mins=8,
 title="How Much Does 24-Hour Care Cost in 2026? Real Numbers & How to Pay",
 desc="2025 national median costs for in-home care, assisted living and nursing homes, what drives price, and 9 ways families pay for 24/7 care.",
 body="""
<p>Cost is the first question almost every family asks — and the one most websites dodge. Here are the latest national benchmarks, what really moves the price, and the payment routes worth checking before you commit.</p>
<h2 id="benchmarks">National cost benchmarks (U.S.)</h2>
<p>The figures below are national medians from the CareScout Cost of Care Survey 2025 (published March 2026, 25,000+ rates collected). Your local rates can be 30% higher or lower.</p>
<div class="table-wrap"><table><thead><tr><th>Type of care</th><th>National median</th><th>Approx. monthly</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td>Non-medical in-home care</td><td>$35 / hour</td><td>$6,700 at 44 hrs/week</td></tr>
<tr><td>24-hour in-home care (3 shifts)</td><td>$35 × 24 hrs</td><td>≈ $25,500</td></tr>
<tr><td>Skilled nurse in the home</td><td>$90 / hour</td><td>varies by hours</td></tr>
<tr><td>Adult day health care</td><td>$95 / day</td><td>≈ $2,060 (5 days/wk)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Assisted living</td><td>$6,200 / month</td><td>$6,200</td></tr>
<tr><td>Nursing home, semi-private</td><td>$315 / day</td><td>≈ $9,580</td></tr>
<tr><td>Nursing home, private room</td><td>$355 / day</td><td>≈ $10,800</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
<div class="note">Rule of thumb: once in-home care passes roughly 8–10 hours a day, a residential setting usually costs less. Above 12 hours a day, the gap widens quickly. Run your own numbers with our <a href="{R}cost-calculator.html">care cost calculator</a>.</div>
<h2 id="drivers">What drives the price</h2>
<ul><li><b>Hours and schedule</b> — nights, weekends and holidays often carry premiums.</li><li><b>Care level</b> — companion care costs less than hands-on personal care; skilled nursing costs the most.</li><li><b>Model</b> — live-in care is typically cheaper per day than three awake shifts.</li><li><b>Location</b> — metro areas and high-wage states cost more.</li><li><b>Agency vs private hire</b> — private hires cost less per hour, but you take on payroll, insurance and backup coverage.</li></ul>
<h2 id="pay">9 ways families pay for care</h2>
<ol><li><b>Private funds</b> — savings, pensions, family contributions.</li><li><b>Long-term care insurance</b> — check the elimination period and daily benefit.</li><li><b>Veterans benefits</b> — VA Aid &amp; Attendance and Housebound allowances for eligible veterans and surviving spouses.</li><li><b>Medicaid / waiver programmes</b> — many U.S. states fund home and community-based services for eligible individuals.</li><li><b>Medicare</b> — covers part-time skilled home health and hospice, not ongoing custodial care.</li><li><b>Home equity</b> — sale, rental or (with care) a reverse mortgage.</li><li><b>Life insurance conversions or accelerated benefits.</b></li><li><b>Employer caregiver benefits</b> — some employers offer backup care.</li><li><b>Provincial / national programmes</b> outside the U.S. — e.g. publicly funded home care in Canadian provinces.</li></ol>
<p>Because eligibility rules change, confirm with the programme directly or a certified elder law professional before making financial decisions.</p>
"""),
dict(slug="signs-its-time-for-24-hour-care", cat="Planning", mins=6,
 title="12 Signs It's Time for 24-Hour Care",
 desc="The safety, health and caregiver warning signs that indicate a loved one needs round-the-clock support — and what to do next.",
 body="""
<p>The move to 24-hour care rarely happens on a calendar. It usually follows a pattern of near-misses. If several of these signs sound familiar, it is time to plan — before a crisis makes the decision for you.</p>
<h2 id="safety">Safety signs</h2>
<ol><li>Two or more falls in the last six months, or fear of falling that stops them moving.</li><li>Getting up at night and becoming confused or unsteady.</li><li>Wandering, or trying to leave home at unusual hours.</li><li>Leaving the stove, taps or doors on or unlocked.</li><li>Missing or doubling medications.</li></ol>
<h2 id="health">Health signs</h2>
<ol start="6"><li>Weight loss, dehydration or spoiled food in the fridge.</li><li>Incontinence that is not being managed.</li><li>Repeated hospital or emergency visits.</li><li>A new diagnosis that will progress (e.g. advanced dementia, Parkinson's, ALS, heart failure).</li></ol>
<h2 id="caregiver">Caregiver signs</h2>
<ol start="10"><li>The family caregiver is not sleeping, is ill, or is missing work.</li><li>Family members disagree or feel resentful.</li><li>You are worried every time the phone rings.</li></ol>
<div class="note warn">If there is immediate danger — a fall with injury, chest pain, sudden confusion or signs of stroke — call 911 or your local emergency number first.</div>
<h2 id="next">What to do next</h2>
<ol><li>Write down the specific incidents and times of day they happen.</li><li>Ask the doctor for a functional assessment and any home-health referral.</li><li>Take our <a href="{R}care-quiz.html">care needs quiz</a> to see which care level fits.</li><li>Compare 24-hour shift care, live-in care and residential options with the <a href="{R}cost-calculator.html">cost calculator</a>.</li><li>Request a <a href="{R}get-care.html">free care match</a> to speak with vetted providers.</li></ol>
"""),
dict(slug="live-in-vs-24-hour-care", cat="Planning", mins=5,
 title="Live-In Care vs 24-Hour Care: Which Is Right?",
 desc="A side-by-side comparison of live-in care and 24-hour shift care — supervision, sleep rules, continuity and cost.",
 body="""
<p>Both options put a caregiver in the home around the clock, but they are built for different needs. Choosing wrong either overpays for coverage you do not need, or leaves night-time gaps that are unsafe.</p>
<div class="table-wrap"><table><thead><tr><th></th><th>Live-in care</th><th>24-hour shift care</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td>Caregivers</td><td>1 primary (plus relief)</td><td>2–3 rotating per day</td></tr>
<tr><td>Night coverage</td><td>Present but sleeping</td><td>Awake and alert</td></tr>
<tr><td>Best for</td><td>Sleeps through most nights</td><td>Frequent night needs, high fall risk, late-stage dementia</td></tr>
<tr><td>Continuity</td><td>High — one familiar face</td><td>Moderate — several caregivers</td></tr>
<tr><td>Cost</td><td>Lower daily rate</td><td>Highest — every hour billed</td></tr>
<tr><td>Home needs</td><td>Private bedroom for caregiver</td><td>None beyond care space</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
<h2 id="decide">The one-question test</h2>
<p><b>How many times per night does your loved one need hands-on help?</b> Zero or one: live-in care is usually fine. Two or more, or unpredictable: choose 24-hour shift care. Awake care is also the right choice when someone must be watched continuously (for example, high wandering risk).</p>
<h2 id="hybrid">Hybrid options</h2>
<p>Many families use daytime care plus a few awake overnights per week, or live-in care with an extra awake night caregiver during a difficult period. Care plans should be reviewed every 60–90 days as needs change.</p>
"""),
dict(slug="choosing-a-home-care-agency", cat="Planning", mins=7,
 title="How to Choose a Home Care Agency: 25 Questions to Ask",
 desc="The vetting checklist for home care agencies — licensing, caregiver screening, training, supervision, contracts and red flags.",
 body="""
<p>The agency you choose will send strangers into your family's home, often at night. Treat the selection like hiring a key employee. Use these questions on every call and compare answers in writing.</p>
<h2 id="licensing">Licensing &amp; insurance</h2>
<ol><li>Is the agency licensed where required in your state or province? Ask for the licence number.</li><li>Are caregivers employees (W-2 in the U.S.) or independent contractors?</li><li>Do you carry liability insurance, bonding and workers' compensation?</li></ol>
<h2 id="screening">Caregiver screening &amp; training</h2>
<ol start="4"><li>What background checks do you run, and how often are they repeated?</li><li>Do you verify certifications (CNA, HHA, PSW) and references?</li><li>How many hours of initial and annual training do caregivers complete?</li><li>What dementia-specific training do you provide?</li><li>What is your caregiver turnover rate?</li></ol>
<h2 id="care">Care planning &amp; supervision</h2>
<ol start="9"><li>Who writes the care plan, and is a nurse involved?</li><li>How often is the plan reviewed?</li><li>How do you supervise caregivers in the home?</li><li>How are shifts documented, and can the family see notes?</li><li>Can we meet the caregiver before the first shift?</li><li>What happens if we are not comfortable with a caregiver?</li></ol>
<h2 id="reliability">Reliability</h2>
<ol start="15"><li>What is your policy if a caregiver calls in sick?</li><li>Is there a 24/7 on-call supervisor?</li><li>How quickly can you start and scale hours?</li><li>How do you handle emergencies and hospital transfers?</li></ol>
<h2 id="money">Contracts &amp; billing</h2>
<ol start="19"><li>What are the hourly, overnight, live-in and holiday rates?</li><li>Are there minimum hours per visit or per week?</li><li>What is the cancellation notice period?</li><li>Do you bill long-term care insurance or VA directly?</li><li>Are there deposits or set-up fees?</li><li>How do rate increases work?</li><li>Can we end the contract at any time?</li></ol>
<div class="note warn"><b>Red flags:</b> no written care plan, cash-only payment, pressure to sign the same day, vague answers on background checks, or no after-hours contact.</div>
<p>Download our full <a href="{R}checklist.html">care planning checklist</a> to score agencies side by side.</p>
"""),
dict(slug="dementia-sundowning-tips", cat="Dementia", mins=6,
 title="Sundowning in Dementia: 15 Practical Strategies for Evenings and Nights",
 desc="What sundowning is, common triggers, and practical daytime, evening and night-time strategies caregivers can use.",
 body="""
<p>Sundowning describes increased confusion, agitation or restlessness in the late afternoon and evening for some people living with dementia. It is exhausting for families — but there is a lot you can do.</p>
<h2 id="triggers">Common triggers</h2>
<ul><li>Fatigue and over-stimulation at the end of the day</li><li>Low light and shadows that are misinterpreted</li><li>Hunger, thirst, pain, constipation or needing the toilet</li><li>Disrupted body clock, too much daytime napping</li><li>Caregiver stress that the person senses</li></ul>
<h2 id="day">During the day</h2>
<ol><li>Get morning daylight — a walk or time by a window.</li><li>Keep a predictable routine for meals, activities and rest.</li><li>Limit caffeine and sugar after lunch.</li><li>Keep naps short and early.</li><li>Plan demanding tasks (appointments, bathing) for the morning.</li></ol>
<h2 id="evening">In the evening</h2>
<ol start="6"><li>Turn lights on before dusk to reduce shadows.</li><li>Close curtains to limit reflections.</li><li>Lower noise — switch off busy TV news.</li><li>Offer a calming activity: music they love, folding towels, a photo album.</li><li>Check basic needs: toilet, hunger, pain, temperature.</li></ol>
<h2 id="night">At night</h2>
<ol start="11"><li>Use night-lights along the route to the bathroom.</li><li>Respond to the feeling, not the facts — reassure rather than correct.</li><li>Consider door alarms or motion sensors for wandering.</li><li>Track episodes to spot patterns and share with the doctor.</li><li>Get help: overnight caregivers protect both the person and the family.</li></ol>
<div class="note">A sudden change in behaviour can signal infection (such as a urinary tract infection), pain or a medication effect. Contact a clinician if symptoms appear abruptly.</div>
"""),
dict(slug="fall-prevention-at-home", cat="Safety", mins=5,
 title="Fall Prevention at Home: A Room-by-Room Safety Checklist",
 desc="A practical room-by-room checklist to reduce fall risk for older adults at home, day and night.",
 body="""
<p>Falls are one of the leading causes of injury among older adults, and many happen at home — often at night on the way to the bathroom. These changes are inexpensive and effective.</p>
<h2 id="everywhere">Everywhere</h2>
<ul><li>Remove loose rugs, cords and clutter from walkways.</li><li>Add bright, even lighting and night-lights.</li><li>Keep frequently used items between waist and shoulder height.</li><li>Wear supportive, non-slip footwear indoors.</li></ul>
<h2 id="bathroom">Bathroom</h2>
<ul><li>Install grab bars by the toilet and in the shower.</li><li>Use non-slip mats and a shower chair.</li><li>Consider a raised toilet seat.</li></ul>
<h2 id="bedroom">Bedroom</h2>
<ul><li>Bed height so feet rest flat on the floor when sitting.</li><li>Lamp or touch light within reach of the bed.</li><li>Clear, lit path to the bathroom; consider a bedside commode.</li></ul>
<h2 id="stairs">Stairs &amp; entry</h2>
<ul><li>Sturdy handrails on both sides.</li><li>High-contrast tape on step edges.</li><li>Light switches at top and bottom.</li></ul>
<h2 id="health">Health factors</h2>
<ul><li>Ask a pharmacist to review medications that cause dizziness.</li><li>Annual vision and hearing checks.</li><li>Strength and balance exercise — ask about local programmes or physiotherapy.</li><li>A medical alert device for people who live alone.</li></ul>
<p>If falls are happening at night, overnight care is often the most effective intervention. <a href="{R}services/overnight-care.html">Learn about overnight care</a>.</p>
"""),
dict(slug="caregiver-burnout", cat="Caregivers", mins=6,
 title="Caregiver Burnout: Warning Signs and a 7-Day Recovery Plan",
 desc="Recognise caregiver burnout early and use a practical 7-day plan to get rest, support and respite — without guilt.",
 body="""
<p>Family caregivers provide the majority of long-term care, often while working and raising children. Burnout is not a personal failure — it is a predictable result of too much load and too little recovery.</p>
<h2 id="signs">Warning signs</h2>
<ul><li>Constant exhaustion that sleep does not fix</li><li>Irritability, resentment or feeling numb</li><li>Getting sick more often</li><li>Withdrawing from friends and activities</li><li>Feeling trapped or hopeless</li></ul>
<h2 id="plan">A 7-day reset plan</h2>
<ol><li><b>Day 1 — List everything you do.</b> Every task, every hour. You cannot delegate what you have not named.</li><li><b>Day 2 — Ask for one specific thing</b> from each family member ("Can you cover Tuesday evenings?").</li><li><b>Day 3 — Book respite.</b> Even 4 hours a week of paid care or an adult day programme helps.</li><li><b>Day 4 — Protect sleep.</b> If nights are the problem, price 2 overnight shifts a week.</li><li><b>Day 5 — See your own doctor.</b> Mention your caregiving role.</li><li><b>Day 6 — Join a support group</b> online or locally.</li><li><b>Day 7 — Review.</b> Keep what worked; add one more support.</li></ol>
<div class="note">If you are having thoughts of harming yourself, please reach out now: in the U.S. and Canada call or text 988 (Suicide &amp; Crisis Lifeline), or contact your local emergency number.</div>
"""),
dict(slug="hospital-discharge-checklist", cat="Safety", mins=5,
 title="Hospital to Home: The Discharge Checklist That Prevents Readmission",
 desc="What to ask before discharge, how to prepare the home, and how to organise the first 30 days after a hospital stay.",
 body="""
<p>The weeks after a hospital stay are a vulnerable time. A clear plan before discharge is the single best way to avoid a return trip.</p>
<h2 id="before">Before leaving the hospital</h2>
<ul><li>Get the written discharge plan and a reconciled medication list (new, changed and stopped medications).</li><li>Ask which warning signs mean "call the doctor" vs "call 911".</li><li>Confirm follow-up appointments and who is booking them.</li><li>Ask whether home health, therapy or equipment has been ordered — and when it will arrive.</li><li>Clarify activity limits: lifting, stairs, bathing, driving.</li></ul>
<h2 id="home">Preparing the home</h2>
<ul><li>Set up a recovery space on one floor if possible.</li><li>Install night-lights and clear walkways.</li><li>Arrange a shower chair, raised toilet seat or commode if advised.</li><li>Stock easy, nutritious meals and fluids.</li></ul>
<h2 id="first30">The first 30 days</h2>
<ul><li>Arrange someone to stay for the first 48–72 hours at minimum.</li><li>Keep a daily log: medications, meals, fluids, sleep, pain and symptoms.</li><li>Attend the first follow-up visit within 7–14 days, or as instructed.</li><li>Consider short-term <a href="{R}services/post-hospital-respite-care.html">post-hospital care</a> if family cannot cover all hours.</li></ul>
"""),
]
